WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:54.680
In 1968, astronauts on NASA’s Apollo 8 mission
saw something that should have been impossible.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:01:00.070
In the last moments before the lunar sunrise
and sunset every day, they noticed a haze

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:05.300
developing on the horizon, and then in the
final few seconds before the sun had risen

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:11.190
or fallen, they saw bright bands of light
radiating out from the surface, sharply ascending

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.690
out into the darkness [1].

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.870
On Earth, we enjoy these dazzling rays twice
a day as a result of our atmosphere scattering

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:19.870
sunlight

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.940
But the moon doesn’t have an atmosphere.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:28.610
There are tiny amounts of some noble gases
like helium and argon in the lunar exosphere,

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:34.040
but for all purposes, the space above the
surface of the moon is a vacuum.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.360
So, where did these bands of light come from?

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:44.070
The answer doesn’t lie with atmospheric
gasses, but, instead, dust.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:49.290
The phenomenon observed by Apollo astronauts
was caused by light passing through and being

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:54.770
scattered by layers of microscopic lunar dust
that had been kicked off the surface of the

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:55.770
moon.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.740
For the astronauts of the Apollo missions,
this would have been a beautiful thing to

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.549
witness, but lunar dust has a dark side.

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:08.030
After the end of the final Apollo mission,
Gene Cernan - who was also the last person

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:13.070
to set foot on the moon - said the following:
“I think dust is probably one of our greatest

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.880
inhibitors to a nominal operation on the Moon.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.060
I think we can overcome other physiological
or physical or mechanical problems except

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.060
dust.”

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.430
[2]
From reports taken from the seventeen Apollo

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:29.900
missions as well as analysis of spacesuits,
machinery and equipment after they had returned

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:36.120
to Earth, we now have a very clear picture
of just how damaging lunar dust can be.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.500
It significantly obscured the vision of astronauts
during lunar landings; it caused damage to

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:46.970
pre-existing machinery and objects on the
lunar surface when blown around by the blast

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:52.260
from landing spacecraft; it found its way
into the lunar and command modules, not only

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:58.120
causing eye, nose and lung irritation for
the astronauts, but also covering screens,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.110
damaging electronic equipment and corroding
mechanical switches; it affected and sometimes

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:08.650
complete broke watches, cameras, rovers and
experimental equipment used on the surface

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:13.500
of the moon and found its way into every crevice
in the astronauts spacesuits.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.780
Hose locks and zippers became difficult to
use, the mobility of the suit was reduced,

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.260
life-support system displays were difficult
to see, visors were scratched, electronics

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.790
overheated, and leaks in the suits became
more prevalent, leading to pressure losses.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.430
And even when they tried to remove the dust
with vacuums and brushes in the lunar module,

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:36.790
they were never able to completely get rid
of it, causing the spacesuits to become gradually

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.409
worse over time [3].

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:43.560
The astronauts of Apollo 17 spent the longest
amount of time outside of the lunar module,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:49.680
but only managed 22 hours of Surface EVAs,
during which almost irreparable damage was

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.390
done to the spacesuits - had they conducted
more work on the lunar surface, the suits

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.420
would eventually have been rendered useless..

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:03.390
But 22 hours is only a fraction of what would
be required for any future mission to the

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:04.390
moon.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:10.110
In 2015, NASA laid out its Space Technology
Roadmaps which identified one key and hugely

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.390
ambitious goal [4].

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:17.420
They wanted their spacesuits to be able to
survive for 100 EVAs - a total of 800 hours

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:23.780
spent on the Lunar surface, almost 37 times
the duration the Apollo 17 astronauts spent

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:24.820
outside.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:29.930
More than any other problem facing NASA or
any other space agency, this could be the

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:34.470
making or breaking of a long-term outpost
on the surface of our moon.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:39.070
But how do you begin to extend the life of
a spacesuit by this much?

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:43.940
To get to an answer, we first need to understand
how lunar dust is formed and why it sticks

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.760
to anything it comes in contact with.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:52.140
Unlike the surface of the Earth, the surface
of the moon is under almost constant bombardment

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.140
from small and large meteorites, some of them
only micrometers in diameter.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:02.470
This rain of debris causes parts of lunar
rocks to break off, creating a layer of fine

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:04.770
dust on the lunar surface.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:10.130
Occasionally, these micrometeorite impacts
hit with such force that they melt the minerals

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.700
contained in the soil, turning them into glass.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:19.760
Lunar dust is a mixture of super-fine particles
and razor-sharp glass that’s also incredibly

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.020
dry - there’s no water in the soil or air
to clump the particles together and no natural

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.590
erosion through wind to blunt the sharp edges
[5].

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:33.990
So, this obviously does not mix well with
the intricate mechanisms of the lunar spacesuits.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:38.380
The dust is small enough to get into almost
any gap, and, thanks to the shards of glass

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.320
in the dust, it can easily scratch glass or
metallic surfaces.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.470
But this is only the beginning of the problem.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:50.469
Thanks to the lack of atmosphere or magnetic
field on the moon, lunar dust is exposed to

00:05:50.469 --> 00:05:53.849
every fraction of radiation that’s fired
at it.

00:05:53.849 --> 00:05:58.899
On the side of the moon exposed to the sun,
particles in the dust are hit with X-ray and

00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:03.960
ultraviolet radiation, causing electrons to
be knocked off, creating positive charges

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.380
on the surface of the dust particles.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:10.580
Then, on the side shielded from the sun, the
opposite happens.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:16.469
The particles pick up electrons from solar
winds, causing them to become negatively charged.

00:06:16.469 --> 00:06:21.680
These positive and negative charges on the
particles create electrostatic potentials

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:28.080
on the surface of the moon, reaching up to
20 V on the day side, and an astonishing -3800

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.690
V on the night side.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:36.440
When the Apollo 8 astronauts saw the beautiful
bands of light at sunset and sunrise, they

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:41.339
weren’t seeing the scattering of radiation
through atmospheric gasses, but instead light

00:06:41.339 --> 00:06:46.649
passing through a column of fine dust fired
up from the surface of the moon by electrostatic

00:06:46.649 --> 00:06:51.599
levitation - dust particles of like charges
repel each other, and are repelled by the

00:06:51.599 --> 00:06:57.399
lunar surface, creating a fountain of sharp
microdust that rises above the horizon.

00:06:57.399 --> 00:07:02.300
This is the same dust that Apollo astronauts
walked through and kicked up as they worked

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:07.240
outside the lunar module, sticking to their
suits in the same way a statically-charged

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.620
balloon sticks to your clothes.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:13.920
Charge is one of the main reasons the dust
is so annoying, but it could also be the way

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.420
we get rid of it.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:20.599
In 2021, as part of NASA’s Breakthrough,
Innovative &amp; Game-changing Idea Challenge,

00:07:20.599 --> 00:07:25.570
they tasked university students from across
the world to come up with novel ways of dealing

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:27.289
with the lunar dust problem [6].

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:32.510
Some of the winning solutions included conductive
fibers inspired by chinchilla hair, an electrically

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:37.840
charged brush that could be powered by UV
radiation, and a fabric for spacesuits that

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.959
mimicked the way certain insects use hair-like
structures to collect and deposit pollen.

00:07:42.959 --> 00:07:47.460
All these ideas use different ways to solve
the same problem, but they share a common

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:53.020
approach - using charge to either passively
or actively remove dust from spacesuits.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:57.770
At its simplest, the problem comes down to
this - how do you make the outer layer of

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.660
the spacesuit repel the charged dust instead
of attracting it?

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:05.640
NASA came up with an elegant solution.Instead
of trying to completely redesign the outer

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:11.270
layer of the suit, they decided to create
a system that could be easily integrated into

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:17.300
the pre-existing layers of fabric and material
found in the current generation of spacesuits.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:22.219
Their idea was to create a suit that could
make its own electrical current which would

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:26.210
actively eject dust, acting like some sort
of energy shield.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:30.680
And the first challenge was choosing the conductive
material that the electrodes would be made

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:31.680
from.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.789
This method of dust-ejection wasn’t new.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:38.820
It was inspired by the Electrodynamic Dust
Shield systems th at had been used by NASA

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:44.430
since the late 1960s, on things like solar
panels, cameras and thermal radiators [7].

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:49.740
But in almost all of these cases, the electrodes
- which were made out of silver, copper or

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:54.750
a compound of indium and tin - were placed
on rigid, fixed bodies.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:09:00.070
A spacesuit is designed to move - the outer
layer is made up of segments of overlapping

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:05.850
fabric that roll over each other at the joints
when the astronaut moves their arms or legs.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:10.760
So, while the silver and copper electrodes
used in the pre-existing EDS systems might

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:15.570
be excellent electrical conductors, with their
low elasticity, they wouldn’t survive the

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.880
fatigue caused by the constant flexing of
the joints.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.120
So, a new conductor was needed, one that had
a high electrical conductivity, but that was

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:29.860
flexible enough to withstand the significant
forces exerted on it during extravehicular

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:30.860
activities.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:33.050
And the answer was carbon nanotubes.

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:39.030
These are cylindrical tubes of carbon atoms
stretching only a nanometer across, with walls

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:44.200
one atom thick - like a long, thin layer of
graphene that’s been rolled into a tube.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:49.000
Carbon nanotubes are incredibly strong, with
a considerably higher tensile strength than

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.450
copper, silver or gold and they have a very
high electrical conductivity.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:54.570
\
Animation 9 continued…

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.880
CNTs are also very light.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:01.960
If you compare the mass of different conductors
required to create an Electrodynamic Dust

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:08.380
Shield on the knees, elbows and boot areas
of a lunar spacesuit, around 101 g of copper

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:13.360
wire would be needed, but if you replace the
copper with carbon nanotubes, you only need

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.240
about 16g:

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:19.180
Carbon nanotubes are an excellent choice,
as they could provide high conductivity with

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:25.090
low mass, while also being able to withstand
the 800 hours of EVAs required for future

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:26.470
lunar missions.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:31.630
So, after settling on CNTs, it was then a
matter of finding a way of integrating these

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:35.170
nanotubes into the outer layer of the spacesuit.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:40.730
Lunar spacesuits are made up of up to 21 layers
of material, with an outer orthofabric layer

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:44.920
composed of a mixture of fibers like GORE-TEX
and Kevlar.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:50.740
The carbon nanotube electrodes were made into
yarns and then woven into this outer layer

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:56.490
in a longitudinal direction to limit the tensile
force applied to the electrodes, and they

00:10:56.490 --> 00:11:00.590
were also spaced out from each other to prevent
electrical arcing.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:05.350
The orthofabric in this outer layer acts as
a natural insulator, but there is a chance

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:08.260
it could be broken down by high voltages.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:14.190
Through testing, it was found that the orthofabric
could withstand up to 1200 V, so the dust

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:19.900
removal system was capped at around 1000 V
to prevent dielectric breakdown of the insulating

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:20.900
material.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.220
So, how does the dust removal system actually
work?

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:29.130
The carbon nanotube electrodes are first connected
in parallel, and then activated by a multi-phase

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:35.320
alternating current at voltages between 600
and 1000 V and very low currents, at around

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:36.800
3 mA.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:41.360
This sets up repeating waves of electrical
fields which move across the outer surface

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:42.950
of the spacesuit.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:48.090
As they interact with the charged dust particles,
they add an additional repulsive force to

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:53.060
the particles based on coulomb interactions
- repulsion due to the interaction of two

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:54.760
charged objects.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:59.640
This repulsive force is, overall, greater
than the gravitational and adhesive forces

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:05.430
which stick the particles to the suit, causing
the particle to be ejected from the orthofabric.

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:09.690
But this also works for uncharged particles
that are stuck to the suit.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:14.780
When interacting with a non-uniform electric
field, like in this system, uncharged dust

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:18.880
can be repelled through something called a
dielectrophoretic force..

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:24.890
Overall, the electrical energy of the system
is transformed into mechanical energy as the

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:26.780
dust is kicked off the suit.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:32.130
To test the system, NASA constructed a prototype
knee joint with carbon nanotubes woven into

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:37.500
an outer orthofabric layer, and used it to
measure how much of a lunar dust simulant

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.560
was removed in different scenarios.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:40.560
[8].

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.360
And what they found was remarkable.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:47.640
Through analysis of high resolution images
taken of the fabric before and after activation

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:53.160
of the electrodes, they saw, that up to 96%
of the lunar dust simulant was removed by

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.050
the system (see here for video)
This has enormous potential for use as part

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:58.710
of future moon missions.

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:04.120
The dust removal system could be on continuously
during EVAs, preventing any dust from binding,

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:09.760
or it could be manually activated when an
astronaut noticed a large build up of dust.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:14.540
This dust prevention technology may seem trivial,
but this razor sharp lunar dust is a life

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:17.490
limiting threat to any future moon colony.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:22.750
This kind of simple environment effect, that
requires a breakthrough material to overcome,

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:25.630
is the core of what makes engineering exciting
for me.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:30.650
A relatively simple solution that any new
engineering graduate could have helped develop,

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.150
and go on to see their work land on the moon.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:37.550
Seeing your work go from the lab, to the factory
floor, to the real world is one of the most

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:41.500
satisfying creative processes in the world.

